  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 308 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable conditions continue through \ntonight, with unseasonably warm weather and chances for showers and \nstorms returning later this week into the weekend.\n\nMorning weather analysis shows the Eastern United States under a \nlarge ridge. At the top of the ridge axis at the surface, roughly a \n1028mb high pressure is centered off the New England Coast. This \nhigh continues to lead to mostly clear skies, outside from some high \nlevel cirrus clouds moving across the far SE. Temperatures are \nhovering in the upper 50s and low 60s inland and upper 60s to \nlow 70s along the coast. Through the rest of the morning into \nsunrise expect temperatures to drop as winds are decoupled and \nskies remain clear. Temperatures right before sunrise are \nprogged to be in the low to middle 50s inland and upper 50s to \nlow 60s closer to the coast. In addition, with dew points \nhovering in the upper 50s to low 60s across the southwestern VA\nPiedmont and MD Eastern Shore some patchy ground fog is \npossible by sunrise. Through the rest of the day and into \ntonight the high pressure will start to push south, but will \nremain in control of the weather pattern leading to another \noptimal weather day. With weak SSE flow forecasted today it \nwill lead to high being slightly warmer across the area. \nTemperatures inland will be in low to middle 80s with the higher\ntemperatures located across the Piedmont. While closer to the \ncoast temps will be in the upper 70s to low 80s. For tonight, \ntemperatures are progged to be warmer as dew points slightly \nincrease and some moisture try's to return to the area. Lows \ntonight will be in the upper 50s to low 60s. In addition, with \nthe moisture trying to make a return this will potentially lead \nto patchy fog development especially across NE NC. \n\nLooking ahead to Thursday and Friday, high pressure will be further \nsouth allowing for much warmer temperatures to return to the area \nwith slightly higher dew points. Thursday looks to be the warmest \nday with highs likely in the upper 80s to possible low 90s inland. \nWhile Friday there remains quite a bit of uncertainity in \ntemperatures due to the timing of the front. With the front \nforecasted to come in earlier Friday across the north decided to \nnudge temperatures north of I-64 down a couple of degrees while \nacross the south maintained upper 80s to possible low 90s. In terms \nof rain chances these days, Thursday the piedmont has the potential \nto see a shower or thunderstorms but confidence is low at this time. \nWhile Friday looks to have the overall better chance with the cold \nfront. \n\nAs the week continues to progress the high pressure off the New \nEngland coast will eventually push SW into the Carolina coastal \nplain through midweek. This will likely cause high temps to stay \nmostly in the low-mid 80s through Wed with lows in the 50s to lower \n60s (locally warmer at the immediate coast). However, dew points are \nforecasted to remain very comfortable through at least Thursday. By \nthe end of the week the upper ridge is progged to expand E-NE to the \nTN Valley and central Appalachians. This will likely lead to above \naverage temps to return to the forecast area  with highs in the \nupper 80s and possibly lower 90s. In addition, rain chances look \nmeager through much of the week. The best days for rain chances look \nto be Friday and Saturday as a frontal passage approaches the area. \n\nThen by this weekend Saturday looks slightly cooler as high pressure \nmoves over New England keeping the stalled front south of the area. \nBy SUnday the front will likely lift north bringing chances of \nprecipitation and slightly warmer temperatures back to the area.",
